 Infrastructure Component Breakdown & Why They Were Added1. The Additional Load Balancer (HAProxy Clustered)Why it was added: To completely destroy the Load Balancer Single Point of Failure (SPOF) from our previous architecture.How it works: We pair the original HAProxy server with a brand-new HAProxy Server 4 in an Active-Passive cluster using a tool like Keepalived (VRRP protocol). They share a single Virtual IP (VIP) address. Traffic only hits the Active Load Balancer. If it crashes, the Passive Load Balancer instantly detects the loss of heartbeat, claims the Virtual IP address, and seamlessly assumes routing duties without the client realizing anything happened.2. Splitting Components onto Separate Dedicated ServersInstead of a "mishmash" node architecture where Nginx, the Application runtime, and MySQL fight for resources on the same machine, every layer has been physically isolated:Dedicated Web Servers (Nginx): * Why: Web servers handle high-volume connection multiplexing, SSL/TLS negotiation, and raw file system performance for static assets (images, CSS, JS). By decoupling them, we ensure that a spike in incoming web traffic connections does not steal system resources from the background application layer.Dedicated Application Servers (App Server + Code Base): * Why: Dynamic runtime environments (e.g., Node.js, PHP-FPM, Python WSGI) are highly CPU-intensive because they compile and process business logic. Isolating them means they can leverage full system CPU without choking the web server or the database. It also makes horizontal scaling trivial—if code execution slows down, we can spin up a third application server without deploying new databases or reverse proxies.Dedicated Database Servers (MySQL Primary/Replica): * Why: Databases are incredibly resource-heavy on Memory (RAM) for caching query data and Disk I/O for persistent storage writes. Giving MySQL its own bare metal or virtual machine guarantees that its performance remains stable and its filesystem disk arrays are dedicated purely to data integrity.3. Web Server vs. Application Server: What's the Difference?Understanding why we split these two components comes down to their distinct operational profiles:FeatureWeb Server (e.g., Nginx, Apache)Application Server (e.g., Gunicorn, Puma, PM2)Primary JobAccepts raw HTTP/HTTPS requests directly from clients; serves static files.Executes dynamic backend business logic and runs application code.Content TypeStatic Content (HTML, CSS, JS, images, videos).Dynamic Content (JSON payloads, server-rendered pages, calculated data).Data InteractionDoes not talk to databases; knows nothing about data models.Directly queries and writes data to the Database Cluster.Key ProtocolHandles HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, SSL/TLS certificates.Speaks application-specific protocols (WSGI, FastCGI, reverse-proxied HTTP).Example ScenarioUser visits foobar.com/logo.png $\rightarrow$ Nginx reads it directly from disk and sends it back instantly.User clicks "Buy Now" $\rightarrow$ Nginx proxies request to App Server; App Server runs checkout code and updates database.
